WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.003 --> 00:00:09.175
- That moment when the officer's kneeling
on him, and he's saying, “I can't breathe.”

00:00:09.175 --> 00:00:12.929
And you see the look
in the officer's eyes.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:17.183
And the thing I kept going back
to is, like, this motherfucker

00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:19.728
thinks he's gonna get away with it.

00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:22.731
He thinks there's no repercussions for this.

00:00:22.731 --> 00:00:23.606
- I'm Taylor Rooks,

00:00:23.606 --> 00:00:28.778
and these are conversations about
defining one's relationship to Blackness

00:00:28.778 --> 00:00:31.197
and how that defines their America.

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:35.243
So, JJ, all of these

00:00:35.243 --> 00:00:37.620
begin with the same question.

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:41.791
So my question for you is when
did you realize that being white

00:00:41.791 --> 00:00:43.543
was different from being Black?

00:00:43.543 --> 00:00:45.879
- It's a, it's an interesting
question, for sure.

00:00:45.879 --> 00:00:50.216
So I have to provide a
little bit of background.

00:00:50.216 --> 00:00:53.511
I'm in the middle of five kids.

00:00:53.511 --> 00:00:56.848
We, growing up, we
lived out in the sticks.

00:00:56.848 --> 00:00:59.142
My parents were like hippies.

00:00:59.142 --> 00:01:02.020
I mean, they lived sort of
an alternative lifestyle.

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:04.105
We were in Cookeville,
Tennessee, for a while.

00:01:04.105 --> 00:01:09.110
They had, like, a farm that we
lived on, 11 acres outside

00:01:09.110 --> 00:01:09.903
Charlottesville, Virginia.

00:01:09.903 --> 00:01:15.408
Then we moved to Roanoke, where I
grew up, and I was home-schooled.

00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:18.036
And so in fifth grade, I
started going to public school.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:22.373
And that same year, I also started
playing AAU basketball with

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.167
the city team, the Roanoke Jaguars.

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:30.423
And we had, like, probably 75 percent Black
players and 25 percent white players.

00:01:30.423 --> 00:01:35.512
It was, it was a nice sort of mix of guys.
From that time on, I knew it was different

00:01:35.512 --> 00:01:40.934
because I would have conversations with my
friends on the team that were Black.

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:43.228
And I would have conversations
with my friends on the team

00:01:43.228 --> 00:01:48.775
that were white, and it was immediately
apparent to me that Black people

00:01:48.775 --> 00:01:53.071
are treated differently in this country.
The guys would talk about their aunts

00:01:53.071 --> 00:01:56.950
or their parents or their living
situations, or what it was like at school,

00:01:56.950 --> 00:01:58.743
what it was like to go to the mall.

00:01:58.743 --> 00:02:01.663
And, you know, I'm having these
conversations all throughout elementary

00:02:01.663 --> 00:02:03.832
school now, junior high school.

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:09.254
And then, you know, I went to
Boo Williams when I was 14.

00:02:09.254 --> 00:02:11.506
I played with the 17-year-old group for three years.

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:13.133
He's like an AAU legend.

00:02:13.133 --> 00:02:16.427
So I would drive four-and-a-half
hours across the state and do

00:02:16.427 --> 00:02:18.388
practices and tournaments with them.

00:02:18.388 --> 00:02:23.726
And I think that was, those three years
was really what, like, solidified sort

00:02:23.726 --> 00:02:29.983
of, you know, my, my belief system
on race in America because I had,

00:02:29.983 --> 00:02:32.777
you know, we traveled all
over the world with this team,

00:02:32.777 --> 00:02:36.781
and I just had these amazing and
candid and open conversations with

00:02:36.781 --> 00:02:39.284
guys about race and what it's like.

00:02:39.284 --> 00:02:42.203
- So when you said you guys would have
conversations, like, with your white

00:02:42.203 --> 00:02:46.374
teammates versus your Black teammates,
what was it that’d stick out? Like, when

00:02:46.374 --> 00:02:48.459
the Black teammates would talk
about going to the mall, how was that

00:02:48.459 --> 00:02:51.254
experience different from when the
white teammates would talk about going to the mall?

00:02:51.254 --> 00:02:53.214
- Well, it wasn't even
just the conversations.

00:02:53.214 --> 00:02:54.924
I experienced it with them.

00:02:54.924 --> 00:02:57.010
You know, I can, I can go—

00:02:57.010 --> 00:02:59.470
I went to a county public school.

00:02:59.470 --> 00:03:00.430
I can

00:03:00.430 --> 00:03:05.185
go to the mall as a 13-year-old
or a 14-year-old adolescent

00:03:05.185 --> 00:03:07.353
with two or three white friends.

00:03:07.353 --> 00:03:11.816
And then I can go to the same mall
with my AAU teammates, and how we're

00:03:11.816 --> 00:03:15.069
treated in a store is differently,
how we're looked at is differently.

00:03:15.069 --> 00:03:19.157
When we would stop at restaurants
on the road at AAU tournaments, it’s like

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:22.452
how we're looked at. You can, you
can sense it, you can feel it.

00:03:22.452 --> 00:03:27.999
So I, again, I think that
the, the thing that I've, like,

00:03:27.999 --> 00:03:30.793
continued throughout my adult life—

00:03:30.793 --> 00:03:32.045
um,

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:33.713
and again, I've been very fortunate;

00:03:33.713 --> 00:03:36.799
basketball has given me a lot of
opportunity and has expanded my

00:03:36.799 --> 00:03:39.886
worldview in a lot of different
ways, but especially on race.

00:03:39.886 --> 00:03:43.306
And I've been able to have
these conversations now for,

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:46.059
I mean, jeez, almost 26 years.

00:03:46.059 --> 00:03:48.353
These are conversations that I've
had. And these are conversations that

00:03:48.353 --> 00:03:51.064
I've been able to have in a room with

00:03:51.064 --> 00:03:52.774
a couple white
teammates and a couple Black

00:03:52.774 --> 00:03:53.900
teammates all at the same time.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:57.111
So we've, we've been having
conversations about this

00:03:57.111 --> 00:03:58.988
for, for a long time.

00:03:58.988 --> 00:04:04.327
- So in a time like now—because I've,
I've been very interested in how Black

00:04:04.327 --> 00:04:07.664
people experience this versus how white
people experience this—when you're

00:04:07.664 --> 00:04:12.710
on your phone and you're scrolling
on Twitter, and you see a video—

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.713
- Mhmm.
- Of a Black man being murdered by the police.

00:04:15.713 --> 00:04:16.339
- Yeah.

00:04:16.339 --> 00:04:21.177
- Walk me through your reaction
from the moment you see the video

00:04:21.177 --> 00:04:23.763
to the moment you kind of calm down from seeing the video.
- I'm gonna

00:04:23.763 --> 00:04:28.726
make this personal. So I'm not gonna
make a broad generalization about

00:04:28.726 --> 00:04:32.397
what white people see versus what Black
people see, but I'll make it what I see.

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:35.066
So I have what's called empathy,

00:04:35.066 --> 00:04:37.110
and I have what's called compassion.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:40.154
So I can't watch that video

00:04:40.154 --> 00:04:43.700
through the lens of a Black person
because I'm not a Black person.

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:50.164
But I can try at least, or, or,
you know, you know, try to

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:53.584
comprehend what that would be like.

00:04:53.584 --> 00:04:56.462
It's horrific. It's emotional. It's raw.

00:04:56.462 --> 00:04:57.588
It's visceral.

00:04:57.588 --> 00:04:59.340
It makes you sick to your stomach

00:04:59.340 --> 00:05:03.928
when you see what
happened to George Floyd.

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:10.184
You know, again, that first week
especially, when the emotions were so

00:05:10.184 --> 00:05:13.730
raw, you know, I cried multiple times.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:18.359
And it seemed like every time you
went on Twitter, every time you’d turn

00:05:18.359 --> 00:05:25.491
on the news, you know, it's, it's that
moment when the officer's kneeling on

00:05:25.491 --> 00:05:27.160
him, and he's saying, “I can't breathe.”

00:05:27.160 --> 00:05:30.872
And you see the look
in the officer's eyes.

00:05:30.872 --> 00:05:35.460
And the thing I kept going back to
is, like, this motherfucker thinks

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:37.628
he's gonna get away with it.

00:05:37.628 --> 00:05:41.299
He thinks there's no
repercussions for this.

00:05:41.299 --> 00:05:42.592
Um,

00:05:42.592 --> 00:05:45.762
and look, if we're talking about,
specifically about police

00:05:45.762 --> 00:05:53.936
brutality, I think there is a culture
where there isn't accountability.

00:05:53.936 --> 00:05:58.274
I think that's been shown time
and time and time again, where

00:05:58.274 --> 00:06:02.362
officers have mistreated

00:06:02.362 --> 00:06:08.701
and sometimes murdered Black Americans,
and there hasn't been any consequences.

00:06:08.701 --> 00:06:10.953
There has not been any consequences.

00:06:10.953 --> 00:06:12.997
And that's the look on his face.

00:06:12.997 --> 00:06:15.166
I know there's not a consequence to this.

00:06:15.166 --> 00:06:17.418
There's a smirk to it.

00:06:17.418 --> 00:06:22.090
There's this, there's this notion
of I'm more powerful than you.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.426
And that, that makes you sick.

00:06:25.426 --> 00:06:26.844
It makes you sick.

00:06:26.844 --> 00:06:29.013
That's not how it should be.

00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:30.807
That's not the world I wanna be in.

00:06:30.848 --> 00:06:34.519
That's not the society that I
wanna raise my children in.

00:06:34.519 --> 00:06:39.023
So, yeah, it's, it's emotional.
It's emotional for anybody.

00:06:39.023 --> 00:06:44.821
And, and, you know, obviously I've
talked to a lot of people over the last

00:06:44.821 --> 00:06:47.990
few weeks, and I've talked to a lot of
people from a lot of different races.

00:06:47.990 --> 00:06:50.660
It's emotional for everyone.

00:06:50.660 --> 00:06:51.494
Um,

00:06:51.494 --> 00:06:54.747
and again, I just go back to, like,
trying to view it through the lens

00:06:54.747 --> 00:06:58.376
of a Black American and what that
must be like, and it's difficult to

00:06:58.376 --> 00:07:01.504
comprehend, but it just makes you sick.

00:07:01.504 --> 00:07:03.923
- I wanna talk about those
emotions cuz everything you said,

00:07:03.923 --> 00:07:05.633
I absolutely agree with.

00:07:05.633 --> 00:07:08.177
So for me as I go through this—
- Yeah.

00:07:08.177 --> 00:07:18.604
- The emotions that I feel are hurt,
disposable, insignificant.

00:07:18.604 --> 00:07:24.652
Kind of cast aside, frustrated,
sick, tired. And those are all emotions

00:07:24.652 --> 00:07:28.197
because when I look at the video, I see myself.
- Sure.

00:07:28.197 --> 00:07:36.247
- What are the exact emotions, the words that you
feel when you see those videos? Because I just

00:07:36.247 --> 00:07:41.961
wonder—I agree with you that it is
impossible for a white person to fully

00:07:41.961 --> 00:07:44.464
feel that; it will never be possible.

00:07:44.464 --> 00:07:44.755
- Yeah.

00:07:44.755 --> 00:07:46.966
- But so is it

00:07:46.966 --> 00:07:47.884
sadness?

00:07:47.884 --> 00:07:48.926
Is it guilt?

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:51.554
Is it, like, what are
the things that you see?

00:07:51.554 --> 00:07:53.514
Cuz I wanna understand that as well.

00:07:53.514 --> 00:07:54.474
- It's a great question.

00:07:54.474 --> 00:07:57.643
I actually have a follow-up question
to you about what you just described.

00:07:57.643 --> 00:08:02.690
So, so first of all, for
me, sadness, for sure.

00:08:02.690 --> 00:08:03.691
Um,

00:08:03.691 --> 00:08:05.151
guilt, for sure.

00:08:05.151 --> 00:08:07.945
I don't wann call it white guilt,
but I will say it's guilt, and I'll

00:08:07.945 --> 00:08:12.158
tell you why. Because I feel like

00:08:12.158 --> 00:08:17.830
if, if I'm in a society where that is
allowed to happen in broad daylight,

00:08:17.830 --> 00:08:21.709
where there's a modern-day lynching in
broad daylight in what is supposed

00:08:21.709 --> 00:08:25.963
to be a progressive city in this
country, then I'm not doing enough.

00:08:25.963 --> 00:08:27.340
That's where the guilt for me is.

00:08:27.340 --> 00:08:28.257
I'm not doing enough.

00:08:28.257 --> 00:08:29.217
I haven't done enough.

00:08:29.217 --> 00:08:33.137
The people I know that have
an influence and have a voice,

00:08:33.137 --> 00:08:34.764
they haven't done enough.

00:08:34.764 --> 00:08:39.227
The, the overriding emotion for me was anger.

00:08:39.227 --> 00:08:44.023
And so I wanted to ask you about this because
I think the anger for me and the anger for

00:08:44.023 --> 00:08:45.858
a Black person is two different things.

00:08:45.858 --> 00:08:51.489
- Yeah, no, I think it's such a deep-rooted
anger for Black people because it's the

00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:57.703
kind of anger that is always there because
it feels like nothing at all is changing.

00:08:57.703 --> 00:09:00.289
Um,

00:09:00.289 --> 00:09:04.877
and realizing at some points that
you feel like you aren't in control

00:09:04.877 --> 00:09:08.673
of your life. More importantly,
you're not gonna be in control of

00:09:08.673 --> 00:09:14.011
your death and what the consequences
of that death probably won't be.

00:09:14.011 --> 00:09:17.348
And I always think of, there's
this book and it's called On Earth

00:09:17.348 --> 00:09:18.224
We're Briefly Gorgeous.

00:09:18.224 --> 00:09:22.186
And the author in it, he talks about
hummingbirds, and he says looking

00:09:22.186 --> 00:09:26.857
at a hummingbird moving so quickly
but staying in the same place.

00:09:26.857 --> 00:09:29.860
I think that's what Black people feel
like, like a bunch of hummingbirds that

00:09:29.860 --> 00:09:34.699
are having to do so much, but they're
not moving, and they're screaming

00:09:34.699 --> 00:09:37.618
so loud, but no one is listening.

00:09:37.618 --> 00:09:40.871
I mean, I have been angry
to the point that has driven

00:09:40.871 --> 00:09:42.999
me to, like, hate. I don’t mean—

00:09:42.999 --> 00:09:43.374
- Yes.
- Obviously

00:09:43.374 --> 00:09:44.000
I don't mean hate all white people.
- OK.

00:09:44.000 --> 00:09:45.418
- But I mean I hate

00:09:45.418 --> 00:09:47.378
the person I see in that video.

00:09:47.378 --> 00:09:48.170
- Sure.

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:52.008
- Think something bad should happen
to that person in that video

00:09:52.008 --> 00:09:55.052
for hurting this person who didn't deserve it.
- Yes.

00:09:55.052 --> 00:10:00.641
- So, like, there has to be a word that
is deeper than anger, you know?
- Right.

00:10:00.641 --> 00:10:03.811
- And I think it's also scary sometimes
cuz I know that a lot of Black

00:10:03.811 --> 00:10:07.106
people also feel incredibly numb to this.

00:10:07.106 --> 00:10:12.737
Like, I have watched so many people that
could have been my friend die on my phone.

00:10:12.737 --> 00:10:15.698
Like, normal-day scrolling, and then
they're shot and they're dead and

00:10:15.698 --> 00:10:18.284
they're not ever gonna come back.

00:10:18.284 --> 00:10:18.951
- Yeah.

00:10:18.951 --> 00:10:20.703
- And it could have really been anybody.

00:10:20.703 --> 00:10:25.291
It could have been any of us. This not
being able to do the simplest things.

00:10:25.291 --> 00:10:30.421
So yeah, it's anger, but, like,
it's fear too, because the world,

00:10:30.421 --> 00:10:35.051
because America has created this
system where this white person is

00:10:35.051 --> 00:10:37.762
going to be more powerful than me.

00:10:37.762 --> 00:10:39.430
And that's fear.
- Yeah.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.516
- So I feel scared when I watch
the videos, and I feel very

00:10:42.516 --> 00:10:44.393
angry when I watch the videos.

00:10:44.393 --> 00:10:47.980
- So you mentioned the word, like, numb.
- Mhmm.

00:10:47.980 --> 00:10:56.447
- And so I think what is really sad to
me is the notion that a Black person

00:10:56.447 --> 00:10:59.533
can be so angry at that video

00:10:59.533 --> 00:11:03.245
while simultaneously feeling

00:11:03.245 --> 00:11:08.584
such a sense of despair, numbness,
coldheartedness, because as you just

00:11:08.584 --> 00:11:14.131
described, they feel like a hummingbird
moving a million miles an hour in place.

00:11:14.131 --> 00:11:16.634
And that is, that's sad.

00:11:16.634 --> 00:11:17.635
That's really sad to me.

00:11:17.635 --> 00:11:19.553
That's hard for me to
sort of reconcile.

00:11:19.553 --> 00:11:20.471
It really is.

00:11:20.471 --> 00:11:23.015
- Yeah, no, I think absolutely.

00:11:23.015 --> 00:11:29.230
And it's, it's hard to live that way.
- Because there needs to be a, there needs to be—

00:11:29.230 --> 00:11:32.692
you would hope in a progressive society,
you would hope that there could be a

00:11:32.692 --> 00:11:36.862
way to use that anger in a positive way.

00:11:36.862 --> 00:11:37.571
- Yeah, for sure.

00:11:37.571 --> 00:11:39.824
- So it's like I'm angry.

00:11:39.824 --> 00:11:45.246
That emotion when it subsides, if
it turns into a sense of despair,

00:11:45.246 --> 00:11:49.291
a sense of numbness, a sense of
coldheartedness, then where have we gotten?

00:11:49.291 --> 00:11:52.586
We've gotten to the same place that we just were.
- Agreed. Now,

00:11:52.586 --> 00:11:57.174
and I think—so I saw this tweet, and it
was from a man named Dwayne Reed, and he

00:11:57.174 --> 00:12:02.847
said, “White supremacy won't die until
white people see it as a white issue they

00:12:02.847 --> 00:12:05.391
need to solve rather than a Black issue

00:12:05.391 --> 00:12:06.851
they need to empathize with.”

00:12:06.851 --> 00:12:07.476
- No doubt.

00:12:07.476 --> 00:12:11.897
- And I do think a lot of white people are
feeling like, Well, I feel sad for you.

00:12:11.897 --> 00:12:12.606
I'm empathizing.

00:12:12.606 --> 00:12:13.232
I'm seeing you.

00:12:13.232 --> 00:12:17.278
And, like, that's the role that I play,
when really the role that white people

00:12:17.278 --> 00:12:20.406
play is dismantling the entire thing.
- Totally agree.

00:12:20.406 --> 00:12:24.618
- And everybody has to feel that way for
there to be, like, any movement at all.

00:12:24.618 --> 00:12:27.705
You know, the talking about it

00:12:27.705 --> 00:12:29.123
isn't the work.

00:12:29.123 --> 00:12:32.793
You know, actively seeing it in
your life and fixing it is the work.

00:12:32.793 --> 00:12:33.711
- Right.

00:12:33.711 --> 00:12:36.839
- And I also don't think
listening is the work.

00:12:36.839 --> 00:12:39.133
I think it's important to
listen, but it isn't enough to

00:12:39.133 --> 00:12:40.134
be like, “I wanna hear you.”

00:12:40.134 --> 00:12:44.805
It's like, figure out the things that
you can do independently, the things

00:12:44.805 --> 00:12:49.018
that you can do with your family
when you go home to try to change this

00:12:49.018 --> 00:12:54.148
trend that has been here forever.

00:12:54.148 --> 00:12:57.818
So I think, yeah, people's roles

00:12:57.818 --> 00:13:02.490
are very different, and the burden
isn't on the oppressed. It's on

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:05.701
the oppressor. And you just, that's—

00:13:05.701 --> 00:13:09.622
I think it's very important to
always make that distinction.

00:13:09.622 --> 00:13:15.002
But I do know, obviously, this is a
super nuanced and complex conversation.

00:13:15.002 --> 00:13:17.755
We talk about family.

00:13:17.755 --> 00:13:24.303
How do you, or have you, or are you going
to speak to your kids about race?

00:13:24.303 --> 00:13:25.262
- It's a great question.

00:13:25.262 --> 00:13:28.307
So I just wanna go back to what you said just a second ago.
- Yeah.

00:13:28.307 --> 00:13:32.853
- So, so first of all, you are
absolutely correct in that the system

00:13:32.853 --> 00:13:36.524
was created by white people, and
it's going to have to be white

00:13:36.524 --> 00:13:38.526
people that dismantle the system.

00:13:38.526 --> 00:13:39.735
I agree with you.

00:13:39.735 --> 00:13:45.449
I think when you mentioned, like,
listening is not the action, I would

00:13:45.449 --> 00:13:50.830
just say to white people specifically,
it's part of the action, because if

00:13:50.830 --> 00:13:54.834
you don't know what the conversation
is about, then you can't help. And

00:13:54.834 --> 00:13:59.964
you've seen again and again, where
there's been some well-intentioned,

00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:03.467
well-meaning white people who genuinely
I think are good people who wanna

00:14:03.467 --> 00:14:07.388
help, but they've been a little tone-deaf
because they're not listening

00:14:07.388 --> 00:14:09.390
to what the conversation is about.

00:14:09.390 --> 00:14:12.142
And then the second part of
listening to me is acknowledgement.

00:14:12.142 --> 00:14:15.145
So it's, it's listening
and then acknowledging.

00:14:15.145 --> 00:14:19.984
And then we can start taking
action cuz we need to know—

00:14:19.984 --> 00:14:21.819
“we” meaning “white people”—

00:14:21.819 --> 00:14:23.988
we need to know exactly what
we're trying to solve here.

00:14:23.988 --> 00:14:26.824
Cuz not all of us created the system,
and I'm not saying I'm not to blame.

00:14:26.866 --> 00:14:30.995
What I'm saying is I'm not the, I
didn't vote for all the policy people.

00:14:30.995 --> 00:14:35.332
You know, the systemic
racism that has been around

00:14:35.332 --> 00:14:40.713
since slavery ended, obviously it's
slavery for 250 years, was even worse

00:14:40.713 --> 00:14:44.842
than systemic racism, but—
- For sure. Yeah. It's not that white people are the problem.

00:14:44.884 --> 00:14:47.344
It's that racism is.
- No, no, no. 100 percent.

00:14:47.344 --> 00:14:47.469
- Yeah.

00:14:47.469 --> 00:14:49.471
And I'm not saying you're saying it. I agree.

00:14:49.471 --> 00:14:54.101
Like, it's not white people that
are the issue. Racism is the issue.

00:14:54.101 --> 00:14:55.561
And the only

00:14:55.561 --> 00:14:58.105
thing I'd say I have a
problem with listening—

00:14:58.105 --> 00:15:00.274
I agree listening absolutely is a part of it—
- Yeah, sure.

00:15:00.274 --> 00:15:03.861
But I think sometimes people equate listening

00:15:03.861 --> 00:15:11.744
and hearing. It isn't enough to sit down
and just, like, listen to the Black person.

00:15:11.744 --> 00:15:14.288
It's then translating that
into your everyday life.

00:15:14.288 --> 00:15:19.001
So it's like, what actionable item are
you gonna take from the listening?

00:15:19.001 --> 00:15:22.212
Is why I'm like it doesn't
just stop with listening.

00:15:22.212 --> 00:15:22.755
Listening,

00:15:22.755 --> 00:15:27.551
listening is important because
listening is step one, and then, you

00:15:27.551 --> 00:15:28.719
know, step two and step three

00:15:28.719 --> 00:15:32.556
and step four is where I think that
change comes, but you're absolutely right.

00:15:32.556 --> 00:15:35.976
You have to listen to
figure out what's next.

00:15:35.976 --> 00:15:36.310
- Yeah.

00:15:36.310 --> 00:15:43.776
- But a lot of attention has to be put on, put on what's next.
- For sure.

00:15:43.776 --> 00:15:47.321
So the family question that you just asked.

00:15:47.321 --> 00:15:54.912
So, I already have—and not just
in the last three weeks—I've already

00:15:54.912 --> 00:15:57.373
had these conversations with my kids.

00:15:57.373 --> 00:15:59.541
We already talk about these things.

00:15:59.541 --> 00:16:03.545
You know, especially with
the older one who is

00:16:03.545 --> 00:16:07.925
fairly precocious and sort of gets
things and has always had sort of a good

00:16:07.925 --> 00:16:10.511
understanding and is very observational.

00:16:10.511 --> 00:16:11.971
Um,

00:16:11.971 --> 00:16:16.642
you know, even with, you know—
it was a, it was a hard day

00:16:16.642 --> 00:16:21.355
the day after the George Floyd
video came out cuz as parents,

00:16:21.355 --> 00:16:22.523
you don't want your kids to see that.

00:16:22.523 --> 00:16:24.525
Well, my son, my oldest—he's
getting ready to turn six—he

00:16:24.525 --> 00:16:25.859
saw it.

00:16:25.859 --> 00:16:27.152
- Wow.

00:16:27.152 --> 00:16:27.945
Almost six.

00:16:27.945 --> 00:16:28.612
And he saw it.

00:16:28.654 --> 00:16:29.071
- Yeah.

00:16:29.071 --> 00:16:33.117
And we watched, we let them
watch cartoons in the morning.

00:16:33.117 --> 00:16:35.035
They watched a cartoon show
while they had breakfast.

00:16:35.035 --> 00:16:37.997
And then at like 7:30, 8:00,
we put on The Today Show.

00:16:37.997 --> 00:16:41.542
And so the video was on, it was
just on. And he's in the room still.

00:16:41.542 --> 00:16:46.588
So he says, um, he said, “Mom,
I don't understand why that

00:16:46.672 --> 00:16:49.174
police officer killed that man.

00:16:49.174 --> 00:16:50.592
Why did he kill that man?

00:16:50.592 --> 00:16:52.761
Police officers are good.

00:16:52.761 --> 00:16:55.556
You know, police officers are good.”

00:16:55.556 --> 00:16:59.309
That's, that's his worldview based
on whatever television—I mean,

00:16:59.309 --> 00:17:01.854
I don't talk to him about police
officers—whatever television

00:17:01.854 --> 00:17:03.313
he watches, whatever it is,

00:17:03.313 --> 00:17:05.149
that's his worldview. Police officers are good.

00:17:05.149 --> 00:17:05.899
Good guys.

00:17:05.899 --> 00:17:07.735
There's good guys and
bad guys in everything

00:17:07.735 --> 00:17:08.318
he watches.

00:17:08.318 --> 00:17:08.652
Good guys.

00:17:08.652 --> 00:17:10.029
Bad guys.

00:17:10.029 --> 00:17:14.742
So having to explain to him
that not all police officers are

00:17:14.742 --> 00:17:16.869
good, that police officers are

00:17:16.869 --> 00:17:21.206
inherently meant to protect people and
inherently meant to serve people, but

00:17:21.206 --> 00:17:23.375
that isn't always the case.

00:17:23.375 --> 00:17:25.502
And to try to explain
that to a six-year-old is not

00:17:25.502 --> 00:17:27.212
the easiest thing in the world.

00:17:27.212 --> 00:17:28.589
Um,

00:17:28.589 --> 00:17:34.720
you know, I'm just gonna be candid
with you about the idea of

00:17:34.720 --> 00:17:36.638
teaching your kids about race.

00:17:36.638 --> 00:17:40.267
So racism is in itself is
a learned behavior, right?

00:17:40.267 --> 00:17:44.188
So I think a lot of people

00:17:44.188 --> 00:17:46.440
say, like, “I'm colorblind.”

00:17:46.440 --> 00:17:48.108
It's not an actual thing.

00:17:48.108 --> 00:17:49.318
Like, we're human beings.

00:17:49.318 --> 00:17:53.197
We, we make distinctions
and create categories.

00:17:53.197 --> 00:17:55.657
It's from an evolutionary
standpoint. We've always done this.

00:17:55.657 --> 00:17:58.160
This is not, like, a new thing.

00:17:58.160 --> 00:18:02.206
So the conversation that I
have is, is—you know, I'll be very

00:18:02.206 --> 00:18:03.999
frank with you—to a five-year-old,

00:18:03.999 --> 00:18:08.045
here's what I tell my five-year-old:
“You treat everyone the same, no matter

00:18:08.045 --> 00:18:11.298
what they look like, no matter what
they talk like, no matter how they

00:18:11.298 --> 00:18:14.760
move, and no matter how they treat
you. You treat everybody the same.”

00:18:14.760 --> 00:18:19.473
And, you know, we had a chance during
the first few weeks of quarantine

00:18:19.473 --> 00:18:22.184
when they were still in school,
we were homeschooling every day.

00:18:22.184 --> 00:18:24.520
So we would do a word of the week.

00:18:24.520 --> 00:18:25.562
Um,

00:18:25.562 --> 00:18:30.734
and the first, the first word
that we learned was respect.

00:18:30.734 --> 00:18:33.946
And we went through every day, we
went through the different meanings

00:18:33.946 --> 00:18:36.949
of what it meant to respect, you know,
and in a very small way, you

00:18:36.949 --> 00:18:40.702
respect your parents, you respect your
family members, you respect your brother,

00:18:40.702 --> 00:18:41.954
maybe treat your brother with respect.

00:18:41.954 --> 00:18:43.122
How does that look,

00:18:43.122 --> 00:18:43.872
right?

00:18:43.872 --> 00:18:46.834
Then it was, how do you treat
other people with respect?

00:18:46.834 --> 00:18:52.589
Obviously, there's only so much you
can teach your five- and three-year-olds.
- For sure.

00:18:52.589 --> 00:18:54.550
- But these are things we're already doing.

00:18:54.550 --> 00:18:56.802
- Mhmm.

00:18:56.802 --> 00:18:58.512
- You know, I'm a Christian.

00:18:58.512 --> 00:19:01.265
I subscribe to this guy Richard Rohr.

00:19:01.265 --> 00:19:04.810
He's an incredible, incredible
thinker, incredible philosopher.

00:19:04.810 --> 00:19:06.061
He's a Franciscan priest.

00:19:06.061 --> 00:19:11.108
He works at this, at this
center out in New Mexico.

00:19:11.108 --> 00:19:13.610
And his worldview is the
same worldview that I have.

00:19:13.610 --> 00:19:15.529
And it's the same worldview that Jesus had.

00:19:15.529 --> 00:19:20.784
And it's the same worldview that I want
my kids to have, which is basically,

00:19:20.784 --> 00:19:25.581
you love everybody and you love God,
and everything else stems from that.

00:19:25.581 --> 00:19:27.958
That's a very simplified version.

00:19:27.958 --> 00:19:30.460
And obviously the world is way
more complicated than that.

00:19:30.460 --> 00:19:33.463
But if that's your building
block and that's your foundation,

00:19:33.463 --> 00:19:34.423
then it's really hard,

00:19:34.423 --> 00:19:39.678
I think, to allow sort of those,

00:19:39.678 --> 00:19:42.431
that evilness to creep in.
- Mhmm. For sure.

00:19:42.431 --> 00:19:43.265
And you're right.

00:19:43.265 --> 00:19:48.729
You know, I, I can't imagine how
you would frame that to somebody

00:19:48.729 --> 00:19:54.693
that is five, you know?
- There's only so much developmentally a kid—
- For sure. But

00:19:54.693 --> 00:20:00.449
obviously, I think, I think reflected
in your life, as you got older, you

00:20:00.449 --> 00:20:05.162
were taught that in order to teach
someone about respect, you also have

00:20:05.162 --> 00:20:09.291
to teach them about Black and white
and how those things are related.
- Yes. For sure. For sure.

00:20:09.291 --> 00:20:11.877
- So how did your parents

00:20:11.877 --> 00:20:14.338
do that to you or teach that to you?

00:20:14.338 --> 00:20:16.840
Or how did you learn yourself
if it wasn't from your parents?

00:20:16.840 --> 00:20:18.217
- No, it was from my parents.

00:20:18.217 --> 00:20:22.054
Look, we, we grew up in, I would
consider where we grew up the South.

00:20:22.095 --> 00:20:25.265
You know, if, if you live in
Virginia and you live in the D.C.

00:20:25.265 --> 00:20:27.142
area, you're not from the South,

00:20:27.142 --> 00:20:27.601
OK?

00:20:27.601 --> 00:20:31.021
I'm not from the Deep South, but I
am from the South. Southern Virginia—

00:20:31.021 --> 00:20:32.356
- No, for sure.
- Tennessee, like, it's the South.

00:20:32.397 --> 00:20:36.985
And obviously some of these, um,
these thought processes

00:20:36.985 --> 00:20:38.528
are embedded in the culture.

00:20:38.528 --> 00:20:42.658
You know, we used to go down to
North Carolina for AAU tournaments, or

00:20:42.658 --> 00:20:45.535
when I would drive down from Roanoke to
Duke, you had to pass through this

00:20:45.535 --> 00:20:50.332
town in Franklin County, which
is the county directly south of Roanoke.

00:20:50.332 --> 00:20:52.542
There's this little town
called Boones Mill.

00:20:52.542 --> 00:20:54.086
It was known for two things.

00:20:54.086 --> 00:20:55.921
It was a speed trap.

00:20:55.921 --> 00:21:01.426
And number two, on the side of the
road, there were all these, um, like,

00:21:01.426 --> 00:21:07.057
antique, you know, knickknack goods
stores with Confederate flags everywhere.

00:21:07.057 --> 00:21:12.396
So you were reminded of that evilness,
again, evilness, you're reminded of that

00:21:12.396 --> 00:21:14.231
every time you drove through the town.

00:21:14.231 --> 00:21:20.445
So even though we were from the South,
my parents, you know, they grew up

00:21:20.445 --> 00:21:26.618
in the North—one in Ohio,
one in Connecticut—met at college,

00:21:26.618 --> 00:21:27.494
um,

00:21:27.494 --> 00:21:32.582
so they had a different worldview than
a lot of people where I was raised.

00:21:32.582 --> 00:21:38.797
And so, you know, again, we were, we were
just taught to love all people, to,

00:21:38.797 --> 00:21:41.717
you know, treat people with respect.

00:21:41.717 --> 00:21:45.095
I've had these conversations with—
my best friend in the world is

00:21:45.095 --> 00:21:47.431
a mix of like four different races,

00:21:47.431 --> 00:21:47.848
OK?

00:21:47.848 --> 00:21:53.228
So we had these conversations all
the time, and I told him the other

00:21:53.228 --> 00:21:58.984
day, I'm like, “You know, growing
up in my household, like, you didn't

00:21:58.984 --> 00:22:02.446
make jokes about race or religion.

00:22:02.446 --> 00:22:05.741
You didn't make jokes about gay people.

00:22:05.741 --> 00:22:11.204
My parents did not raise us that
way.” As an adult, all

00:22:11.204 --> 00:22:12.831
those things are so inappropriate.

00:22:12.831 --> 00:22:14.124
They've always been inappropriate to me.

00:22:14.124 --> 00:22:16.501
They're so inappropriate to me.

00:22:16.501 --> 00:22:17.544
Going back to my kids,

00:22:17.544 --> 00:22:21.131
like, my kids will never
hear me say those things.

00:22:21.131 --> 00:22:21.590
Now,

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:23.759
I'm not doing this to toot my own horn.

00:22:23.759 --> 00:22:31.516
What I'm saying is in some households,
that is loud, and that permeates

00:22:31.516 --> 00:22:36.271
into a mindset of race, right?

00:22:36.271 --> 00:22:41.401
These people above these people,
gays are here, straight are here,

00:22:41.401 --> 00:22:43.362
trans are here, whatever it may be.

00:22:43.362 --> 00:22:44.780
- It creates a status.

00:22:44.780 --> 00:22:45.947
- Yes, exactly.

00:22:45.947 --> 00:22:49.201
And that, my parents
didn't raise me that way,

00:22:49.201 --> 00:22:50.202
didn't raise me that way at all.

00:22:50.202 --> 00:22:56.541
And frankly, like, they allowed me,
you know, to go play with the city team.

00:22:56.541 --> 00:22:59.419
They allowed me in high school to drive
four-and-a-half hours across

00:22:59.419 --> 00:23:04.383
the state to play with Boo Williams
and give me those opportunities.

00:23:04.383 --> 00:23:05.759
There was never any hesitation there.

00:23:05.759 --> 00:23:08.929
It was just, you know, you
wanna be around the best people.

00:23:08.929 --> 00:23:09.846
That's what it came down to.

00:23:09.846 --> 00:23:14.434
- So how do you define racism?

00:23:14.434 --> 00:23:19.648
Because, and I've talked about
this a couple of times on the series,

00:23:19.648 --> 00:23:22.651
some people think racism means pointing to
the Black person, saying, “I don't like you

00:23:22.651 --> 00:23:24.403
cuz you're Black.”
- Mmm, yeah.

00:23:24.403 --> 00:23:28.281
- That's one very specific type of racism.

00:23:28.281 --> 00:23:31.284
And I think one could argue, that's
probably the least threatening type

00:23:31.284 --> 00:23:35.789
of racism. It’s the racism that manifests
itself in the things that we don't see,

00:23:35.789 --> 00:23:42.212
in the systems that we live in every day,
including the police, that are the worst.

00:23:42.212 --> 00:23:44.923
So what is racism

00:23:44.923 --> 00:23:45.882
to you?

00:23:45.882 --> 00:23:51.012
And also, how have you seen maybe white
people you know, white people around

00:23:51.012 --> 00:23:55.183
you, exhibit these racist behaviors?

00:23:55.183 --> 00:23:59.229
- I think racism, it's a, it's
a hard question to answer without

00:23:59.229 --> 00:24:00.105
a dictionary in front of you,

00:24:00.105 --> 00:24:01.940
first of all.
- Yeah.

00:24:01.940 --> 00:24:09.448
- No, but racism to me is
just believing that because of

00:24:09.448 --> 00:24:12.951
your skin color, you are superior

00:24:12.951 --> 00:24:15.245
to a person of a different skin color.

00:24:15.245 --> 00:24:21.168
I think that's a very broad
stroke there, but,

00:24:21.168 --> 00:24:24.004
you know, it encapsulates a bunch of
different feelings, a bunch of

00:24:24.004 --> 00:24:26.339
different real world sort of things.

00:24:26.339 --> 00:24:32.387
The other thing I would say, because
my background in cultural anthropology—

00:24:32.387 --> 00:24:35.599
so in cultural anthropology,
there's this, there's this notion of

00:24:35.599 --> 00:24:39.394
the other, and the other can be
a bunch of different things, but in

00:24:39.394 --> 00:24:42.606
general in culture, the other is

00:24:42.606 --> 00:24:45.567
essentially something
that you see as a threat.

00:24:45.567 --> 00:24:50.113
And I think, you know, it goes back
to something I read the other day

00:24:50.113 --> 00:24:53.617
with Richard Rohr, where he was
talking about the ego lies to us.

00:24:53.617 --> 00:24:58.121
And they think that if someone,
if someone, this group of people

00:24:58.121 --> 00:25:01.791
that they want something that
I have. They want equal rights.

00:25:01.791 --> 00:25:03.460
They want equal protection.

00:25:03.460 --> 00:25:05.253
They want equal opportunity.

00:25:05.253 --> 00:25:07.380
Well, somehow that's gonna take away

00:25:07.380 --> 00:25:12.260
from my rights and my opportunity
when that's just not really how it works.

00:25:12.260 --> 00:25:14.387
The pie is big enough for all of us.
- Sure.

00:25:14.387 --> 00:25:18.975
- So I think, I think going along with that
sort of thinking of superiority, there's

00:25:18.975 --> 00:25:23.021
also an underlying perception of threat.

00:25:23.021 --> 00:25:23.772
- Yeah.

00:25:23.772 --> 00:25:27.025
And I think what you're saying is true.

00:25:27.025 --> 00:25:33.490
I also think the truth of the matter is
one of the reasons racism still exists

00:25:33.490 --> 00:25:36.910
is because white people do recognize

00:25:36.910 --> 00:25:40.705
that they have benefited from that system.

00:25:40.705 --> 00:25:47.671
So it's almost, I don't wanna use the
word beneficial for racism to exist for

00:25:47.671 --> 00:25:52.717
white people, but it has been beneficial
for racism to exist because when you

00:25:52.717 --> 00:25:55.679
put it down to its base level, right,

00:25:55.679 --> 00:26:00.392
if there have been so many movements,
so many people fighting for equal rights

00:26:00.392 --> 00:26:04.521
for so many years, yet it still exists,

00:26:04.521 --> 00:26:08.233
it's because people feel as though
it's necessary for things to continue

00:26:08.233 --> 00:26:10.151
to operate in the way that they do.

00:26:10.151 --> 00:26:11.570
- I totally agree with that.

00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:16.700
I think, I think the group in power
will always want to hold on to

00:26:16.700 --> 00:26:21.246
that power.
- Mhmm.
- And sometimes that can be done in a very subtle way.

00:26:21.246 --> 00:26:24.291
And sometimes that can be
done in a very explicit way.

00:26:24.291 --> 00:26:25.083
Um,

00:26:25.083 --> 00:26:29.337
so if you, you know, if you go back to,

00:26:29.337 --> 00:26:34.884
you know, the war on crime, the war
on drugs, you go back to Bill

00:26:34.884 --> 00:26:36.428
Clinton's crime bill in the ‘90s.

00:26:36.469 --> 00:26:38.638
Like, these were all at the time—

00:26:38.638 --> 00:26:42.058
now we kind of, at least for me, I
didn't perceive that as a 10-year-old

00:26:42.058 --> 00:26:45.729
when Clinton signed that bill in 1994—
but at the time I thought he was like,

00:26:45.729 --> 00:26:47.063
Oh, let's put the bad guys in jail,

00:26:47.063 --> 00:26:48.148
right?

00:26:48.148 --> 00:26:52.527
At the time, you
know, it was, it was maybe

00:26:52.527 --> 00:26:55.614
not as explicit as it seems
now, and now it seems pretty

00:26:55.614 --> 00:26:57.449
explicit, that it was a war on Black people.

00:26:57.449 --> 00:26:58.033
- Yeah.

00:26:58.033 --> 00:26:59.701
Yeah, absolutely agree.

00:26:59.701 --> 00:27:01.703
- Taylor, Taylor, can
I ask you a question?

00:27:01.703 --> 00:27:02.579
- Absolutely.

00:27:02.579 --> 00:27:06.416
- Not, not a book definition, but
to you, what is, what is your,

00:27:06.416 --> 00:27:08.209
what is your definition of racism?

00:27:08.209 --> 00:27:12.839
- I think racism

00:27:12.839 --> 00:27:18.094
is a structure,

00:27:18.094 --> 00:27:20.889
a structure that

00:27:20.889 --> 00:27:24.768
puts one person in power and one
person not in power. For me

00:27:24.768 --> 00:27:28.688
that's—racism is when a white person
is in power and uses that power

00:27:28.688 --> 00:27:30.899
to keep Black people not in power.

00:27:30.899 --> 00:27:35.028
And I don't mean power in the huge term.

00:27:35.028 --> 00:27:40.200
I mean agency. I mean the power
to just be you, the power to do

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:42.077
things you want to do every day.

00:27:42.077 --> 00:27:43.161
So,

00:27:43.161 --> 00:27:43.453
yeah,

00:27:43.453 --> 00:27:49.459
so any time that a white person's power
threatens my existence, that's racism.

00:27:49.459 --> 00:27:50.001
- Yeah.

00:27:50.001 --> 00:27:51.002
- The same way,

00:27:51.002 --> 00:27:53.713
I think any time a person, a
white person's white privilege

00:27:53.713 --> 00:27:56.841
threatens my agency, that's racism.

00:27:56.841 --> 00:28:02.597
Um, whether they are actively
participating in that racism or not, it's

00:28:02.597 --> 00:28:06.434
that racism is so ingrained in everything
that I'm experiencing the racism,

00:28:06.434 --> 00:28:10.438
even though you might not feel like
you're doing something that is racist.

00:28:10.438 --> 00:28:14.234
Because the thing we're living
in creates that environment.

00:28:14.234 --> 00:28:16.569
Does that make sense? - For sure.

00:28:16.569 --> 00:28:21.324
Um, you mentioned, you mentioned agency
by the way, and I've, I've said this, I've

00:28:21.324 --> 00:28:25.495
said this publicly before, but for some
reason, every time I say it, like, people

00:28:25.495 --> 00:28:30.750
freak out. But like, so Kevin Durant in
2016, when he joined the Warriors, LeBron,

00:28:30.750 --> 00:28:36.506
when he makes The Decision, there's
always the group of sports fans who

00:28:36.506 --> 00:28:38.049
want to make the argument,

00:28:38.049 --> 00:28:39.592
“Oh, he took the easy way out.

00:28:39.592 --> 00:28:42.846
You know, he went and joined Dwyane
Wade and Chris Bosh in Miami.

00:28:42.846 --> 00:28:44.681
He went and joined a team

00:28:44.681 --> 00:28:47.600
that had just beat him in
the playoffs.” Like,

00:28:47.600 --> 00:28:51.354
I'm not gonna use any explicitives,
you know, but you know what I'm saying?

00:28:51.354 --> 00:28:54.107
There's that, there's that notion.

00:28:54.107 --> 00:28:58.570
But I think an underlying—I
really believe this—an underlying

00:28:58.570 --> 00:29:06.202
reason for the, sort of the
reaction to Kevin and LeBron making

00:29:06.202 --> 00:29:12.792
those decisions is because people
were uncomfortable with powerful Black

00:29:12.792 --> 00:29:16.588
men making a decision for themselves.

00:29:16.588 --> 00:29:18.381
I really believe that.
- Yeah.

00:29:18.381 --> 00:29:22.218
- And that goes back to

00:29:22.218 --> 00:29:23.762
systemic racism in our society.

00:29:23.762 --> 00:29:25.638
Like, again, it goes back
to what I was saying too.

00:29:25.638 --> 00:29:27.766
It's about the threat, the
perception of a threat.

00:29:27.807 --> 00:29:29.225
Well, the perception: Wait a minute,

00:29:29.225 --> 00:29:32.228
this guy, he's, he's bucking the system.

00:29:32.270 --> 00:29:34.314
- How dare he?

00:29:34.314 --> 00:29:35.356
- How dare he? He's bucking the system.

00:29:35.356 --> 00:29:37.150
- And I think—oh, I'm sorry.

00:29:37.150 --> 00:29:37.859
Didn't mean to cut you off.

00:29:37.859 --> 00:29:41.905
- No, I was just going to say, you know,
when Kevin did that in 2016—this

00:29:41.905 --> 00:29:44.032
is when my podcast was on Yahoo—

00:29:44.032 --> 00:29:45.158
I did a whole episode on this. I did a whole episode.

00:29:45.158 --> 00:29:47.577
I mean, I just fucking loved the decision.

00:29:47.577 --> 00:29:49.078
I loved what he did. Now,

00:29:49.078 --> 00:29:51.539
I was on the Clippers at the time, and
we were trying to beat the Warriors,

00:29:51.539 --> 00:29:54.209
so I was like, “Yeah, well
we're, we're in trouble.”

00:29:54.209 --> 00:29:58.671
But, uh, but I, you know, I loved,
I loved him taking ownership of

00:29:58.671 --> 00:30:02.550
that agency and just saying, “No,
I'm going to do what I want to do.”

00:30:02.550 --> 00:30:03.426
- Yeah.

00:30:03.426 --> 00:30:08.848
And I think that really speaks in a lot of
ways to sports in general, which is, it's

00:30:08.848 --> 00:30:12.101
a lot of Black people making decisions
and a lot of white people telling them why

00:30:12.101 --> 00:30:19.734
that decision maybe was right or wrong.
- Interesting. I’ve never heard it framed that way, but yeah.

00:30:19.734 --> 00:30:23.488
- Which is a lot of what it is.

00:30:23.488 --> 00:30:27.534
I, you know—one thing that I think
is great about both of those guys,

00:30:27.534 --> 00:30:31.704
with LeBron and Kevin, is that they
have done obviously a lot of things

00:30:31.704 --> 00:30:34.082
on the basketball court, but they
have also done so many things for

00:30:34.082 --> 00:30:38.211
Black people, for people in general
in business, et cetera, et cetera.

00:30:38.211 --> 00:30:41.631
Two things with that, though
is, I hate that Black people

00:30:41.631 --> 00:30:45.552
feel as though they have to be

00:30:45.552 --> 00:30:51.140
this excellent being in
order to be considered good,

00:30:51.140 --> 00:30:51.599
right?

00:30:51.599 --> 00:30:56.104
And I think that a lot of Black, I
think a lot of Black people experience

00:30:56.104 --> 00:31:02.068
that—the need to be the best in order
to justify your existence, because white

00:31:02.068 --> 00:31:04.362
people simply do not have to do that.

00:31:04.362 --> 00:31:06.489
I think it's great that
Black people do it.

00:31:06.489 --> 00:31:07.240
I just hate that

00:31:07.240 --> 00:31:12.579
we feel like we have to. That's A.
B, there's always this perceived

00:31:12.579 --> 00:31:16.958
idea that, you know, if you're
rich, you can beat racism.

00:31:16.958 --> 00:31:19.085
If you're educated, you can beat racism.

00:31:19.085 --> 00:31:22.338
If you look this way, you can beat
racism, that all of these things

00:31:22.338 --> 00:31:25.300
will be able to be your armor to
stop white people from not liking

00:31:25.300 --> 00:31:27.260
you simply because you're Black.

00:31:27.260 --> 00:31:28.094
I would argue—

00:31:28.094 --> 00:31:31.931
and this is the case probably for LeBron
and Kevin—that being all three of those

00:31:31.931 --> 00:31:37.270
things in a lot of ways makes you a target
because it goes back to what you talk

00:31:37.270 --> 00:31:42.150
about with the threat or feeling like, "Why
does this person have this when I don't?"

00:31:42.150 --> 00:31:49.532
So while sometimes these conversations
happen, and maybe famous or

00:31:49.574 --> 00:31:52.660
more wealthy Black people are
framed like they have it easy,

00:31:52.660 --> 00:31:57.248
it's important to understand how racism
still exists for the Kevin Durants and

00:31:57.248 --> 00:32:01.002
the LeBron James and every Black person
who was doing something in the public

00:32:01.044 --> 00:32:03.588
eye, because it's absolutely still there.

00:32:03.588 --> 00:32:06.799
It's just showing itself
in, in a different way.

00:32:06.799 --> 00:32:09.594
You know, it's showing itself in
the people that want to burn Kevin

00:32:09.594 --> 00:32:13.014
Durant's jersey because he made a
decision to make his life better.

00:32:13.014 --> 00:32:19.062
- It's uh, it's the, the, the first thing
you said, it's pretty profound actually.

00:32:19.062 --> 00:32:20.355
Um,

00:32:20.355 --> 00:32:26.611
about, about goodness and measuring
up to white people's goodness as if

00:32:26.611 --> 00:32:31.449
that somehow absolves you from racism.

00:32:31.449 --> 00:32:37.538
Um, I mean, and that's the fact that
maybe some people think like that and

00:32:37.538 --> 00:32:41.084
some people feel the need to do that,

00:32:41.084 --> 00:32:45.129
it's, it's incredibly sad, but it just, I
think it speaks volumes—because it's true,

00:32:45.129 --> 00:32:48.007
what you say— it speaks volumes to

00:32:48.007 --> 00:32:52.095
how shitty our society is in that regard.
- Yeah. And it speaks to—

00:32:52.095 --> 00:32:55.181
- Cuz it's not, it's not authentic at all.

00:32:55.181 --> 00:32:57.308
- Yeah.
- It's not authentic

00:32:57.308 --> 00:32:59.727
- Yeah.
- And that the whole system shouldn't be in place

00:32:59.727 --> 00:33:04.983
- It feels like your value
isn't in you being a human.

00:33:04.983 --> 00:33:09.028
Your value is in, like, the
things that you can do.

00:33:09.028 --> 00:33:12.490
And I just don't think,
I think that's a very

00:33:12.490 --> 00:33:17.245
toxic way that sometimes that people
have to live, that you have to

00:33:17.245 --> 00:33:22.750
have things that like, like I said,
justify the space that you take up.

00:33:22.750 --> 00:33:25.920
And it's sad, and it's
more than it being sad,

00:33:25.920 --> 00:33:30.758
it's exhausting, you know. Do I, I
always got to be the best, you know,

00:33:30.758 --> 00:33:35.221
I always got to be way better than
everybody else in this room, just for

00:33:35.221 --> 00:33:37.390
you to think that I belong in the room.

00:33:37.390 --> 00:33:38.057
- Right.

00:33:38.057 --> 00:33:40.393
- But yeah, it is a part of it.

00:33:40.393 --> 00:33:44.647
Um, I want to end with this,
which is the same question that

00:33:44.647 --> 00:33:48.443
I ask everybody at the end, which
is that words mean different

00:33:48.443 --> 00:33:50.111
things to different people.

00:33:50.111 --> 00:33:52.864
How do you define America?

00:33:52.864 --> 00:33:54.323
- So I'm reading this book right now.

00:33:54.323 --> 00:33:58.953
I didn’t know that you were gonna ask me this question.
- It’s right there!

00:33:58.953 --> 00:34:01.748
- So it's written by Colin Woodard.

00:34:01.748 --> 00:34:03.207
So it was written a few years ago.

00:34:03.207 --> 00:34:06.794
This is actually a follow-up to a book
that was written back in the ‘70s.

00:34:06.794 --> 00:34:10.006
I think it was called The
Nine American Nations. Colin

00:34:10.006 --> 00:34:12.300
calls his book, American Nations.

00:34:12.300 --> 00:34:16.637
And he argues that basically
there's 11 nations in our country.

00:34:16.637 --> 00:34:21.893
So with that as sort of the
foundation, I think the easiest

00:34:21.893 --> 00:34:25.813
way to define America is messy.

00:34:25.813 --> 00:34:30.026
The reason it's messy, it's for
a lot of reasons, but there's

00:34:30.026 --> 00:34:32.653
so many competing ideologies,

00:34:32.653 --> 00:34:41.412
there's so many competing agendas in
America because of its history of slavery

00:34:41.412 --> 00:34:46.626
and systemic oppression, especially
of obviously of Black people,

00:34:46.626 --> 00:34:54.008
we can never really, we can never
really be clean. Even if we ever

00:34:54.008 --> 00:34:56.677
reconciled that, we could never be clean.

00:34:56.677 --> 00:35:00.223
America's always going to be messy.

00:35:00.223 --> 00:35:05.520
Now the Constitution, I think,
has done a good enough job

00:35:05.520 --> 00:35:07.897
to keep our society running.

00:35:07.897 --> 00:35:19.617
Um, but when you have someone like
Trump in office who has empowered, um,

00:35:19.617 --> 00:35:25.414
even further empowered
some racist ideology, um,

00:35:25.414 --> 00:35:30.962
and white nationalism, it
makes things really messy.

00:35:30.962 --> 00:35:34.048
And at times it feels untenable.

00:35:34.048 --> 00:35:36.843
At times this society, this
country feels untenable.

00:35:36.843 --> 00:35:41.639
Like we're on the brink. I assume—

00:35:41.639 --> 00:35:42.431
I’m, I’m

00:35:42.431 --> 00:35:44.892
talking about everybody, white
people, Black people, everybody—

00:35:44.892 --> 00:35:48.980
I assume that we've all
felt this way before.

00:35:48.980 --> 00:35:55.069
And whether that was, you know,
in the late ‘60s, World War II,

00:35:55.069 --> 00:35:56.362
um,

00:35:56.362 --> 00:36:00.158
obviously during the Civil War, but
if you look, it's funny, cuz if you

00:36:00.158 --> 00:36:02.785
look at all the times that our society

00:36:02.785 --> 00:36:06.038
and our country has really been at odds,

00:36:06.038 --> 00:36:09.667
it's been centered around race.

00:36:09.667 --> 00:36:12.628
So that's, that's my
definition. Messy, messy.

00:36:12.628 --> 00:36:14.213
America's messy.

00:36:14.213 --> 00:36:14.964
- That was thoughtful.

00:36:14.964 --> 00:36:17.758
- And a lot of, and a lot of it is, is race.

00:36:17.758 --> 00:36:18.926
- And yeah, you said something true.

00:36:18.926 --> 00:36:21.137
You're like, you know, so
many things have happened.

00:36:21.137 --> 00:36:26.601
I don't know if it would ever be completely
not messy, but I hope that everybody

00:36:26.601 --> 00:36:30.980
can do their part to just clean it
up as much as, as much as possible.

00:36:30.980 --> 00:36:32.064
For sure.
- Yeah.

00:36:32.064 --> 00:36:34.150
Look, we need to do a better job.

00:36:34.150 --> 00:36:39.780
We, as in sort of our generation,
we need to do a better job

00:36:39.780 --> 00:36:42.658
of participating in all elections.

00:36:42.658 --> 00:36:47.163
And I just want to give you some
credit because you recently voted, uh,

00:36:47.163 --> 00:36:51.500
in Georgia in the primaries, right?
- Yes! Vote! Yes! It’s so important!

00:36:51.500 --> 00:36:53.586
- I just want to just say
thanks for doing that.
- Thank you.

00:36:53.586 --> 00:36:59.300
- Um, you know, I've, I've, I've voted in
the national election since 2004 and in

00:36:59.300 --> 00:37:04.305
some state elections, but I’ve, I've never
been consistent with local elections.

00:37:04.305 --> 00:37:05.932
And so this is, going forward,

00:37:05.932 --> 00:37:10.228
this is sort of one of my, you know, touch
points for me about how can I be better,

00:37:10.228 --> 00:37:10.978
right?

00:37:10.978 --> 00:37:12.939
And so that's one of them.

00:37:12.939 --> 00:37:13.856
Um,

00:37:13.856 --> 00:37:18.194
and the other thing is, you
know, what's really interesting

00:37:18.194 --> 00:37:20.112
that’s happening right now.

00:37:20.112 --> 00:37:23.074
Like, we have so far to go,
Taylor, you and I could agree.

00:37:23.074 --> 00:37:27.036
We have so far to go. But for
me, I'm trying to be an optimist,

00:37:27.036 --> 00:37:28.329
and I'm trying to be an optimist.

00:37:28.329 --> 00:37:31.082
It's been three weeks or so,

00:37:31.082 --> 00:37:35.920
it’s been three weeks or so since, since
George Floyd was murdered, and

00:37:35.920 --> 00:37:40.591
we're already seeing action,
we're already seeing some action.

00:37:40.591 --> 00:37:43.135
You know, we've seen local police
departments already and local

00:37:43.135 --> 00:37:45.346
governments already enact some of these

00:37:45.346 --> 00:37:48.933
changes that we've been asking for
with, with no chokeholds, [New York City Mayor Bill] De Blasio

00:37:48.933 --> 00:37:51.519
comes out and he says, we're going
to, we're going to reallocate some

00:37:51.519 --> 00:37:54.146
funds from the NYPD to other areas.

00:37:54.146 --> 00:37:54.897
Um,

00:37:54.897 --> 00:37:55.606
so things are happening.

00:37:55.648 --> 00:37:59.735
I think people are finally
sort of listening. For anybody

00:37:59.735 --> 00:38:00.444
who's listening to this,

00:38:00.486 --> 00:38:01.487
we have to continue.

00:38:01.487 --> 00:38:04.115
We have to keep going, we have to continue.
- Yes. No, I agree.

00:38:04.115 --> 00:38:06.867
- Like, this is not going to
be something that just lasts

00:38:06.867 --> 00:38:07.910
during the summer or something

00:38:07.910 --> 00:38:09.912
that just lasts during COVID.

00:38:09.912 --> 00:38:10.746
Um,

00:38:10.746 --> 00:38:13.291
you know, this is something
that's gotta, it's gotta be,

00:38:13.291 --> 00:38:16.460
you know, years, possibly generations to make it.
We have got to do this.

00:38:16.460 --> 00:38:18.713
I want to end with one thing, and
sorry to be long-winded.

00:38:18.713 --> 00:38:23.426
The first few days after, um,

00:38:23.426 --> 00:38:29.473
after, uh, George Floyd was murdered,
you know, I, again, I, I referred it,

00:38:29.473 --> 00:38:33.269
referred to it earlier as a modern-day
lynching in broad daylight with

00:38:33.269 --> 00:38:36.564
the guy knowing a camera was on him.

00:38:36.564 --> 00:38:40.693
So two of my heroes growing up, you know,
when I was at Duke were, were

00:38:40.693 --> 00:38:44.989
Tommie Smith and John Carlos, and I wrote
two, like, 30-page papers on these guys.

00:38:44.989 --> 00:38:49.910
I had the opportunity last, last fall, the
Pelicans brought in Tommie and Edwin Moses,

00:38:49.910 --> 00:38:53.914
who was another, uh, you know, hero,
amazing athlete, political activist,

00:38:53.914 --> 00:38:56.751
that came in and talked.
- Wait. Just so everyone knows watching if they don't know, I'm

00:38:56.751 --> 00:39:00.338
sure everyone's seen the iconic photo of
two Black men fists up at the Olympics.

00:39:00.338 --> 00:39:03.674
That is who JJ is referring to.
- Tommie wins the gold medal.

00:39:03.674 --> 00:39:08.804
He sets the world record in the 200-meter
Olympic Games in Mexico City in 1968.

00:39:08.804 --> 00:39:11.599
John Carlos wins the bronze.

00:39:11.599 --> 00:39:13.142
And they had agreed upon
doing a couple things.

00:39:13.142 --> 00:39:16.771
So, you know, they, they did
the Black Power fist up.

00:39:16.771 --> 00:39:18.814
John actually forgot his gloves.

00:39:18.814 --> 00:39:22.234
So Tommie gave him his left-handed glove.
- Yup. Oh, I read this story.

00:39:22.234 --> 00:39:24.904
- So John put up his left hand.

00:39:24.904 --> 00:39:28.908
So there was a couple of things that
they were protesting, but so Tom is

00:39:28.908 --> 00:39:34.038
up there with no shoes on, and he's
protesting against, uh, the poverty

00:39:34.038 --> 00:39:35.664
in the, in the Black community.

00:39:35.664 --> 00:39:41.295
John, John Carlos has a, uh, has this, like,
necklace or chain around his neck.

00:39:41.295 --> 00:39:49.387
And he was, he was protesting the
lynching of Black people. That was in 1968.

00:39:49.387 --> 00:39:51.305
It was 52 years ago.

00:39:51.305 --> 00:39:54.058
- Not that long.
- Not that long. Right.

00:39:54.058 --> 00:39:55.309
- Not that long ago. Like,

00:39:55.309 --> 00:39:56.435
that was yesterday.

00:39:56.435 --> 00:39:59.605
- 52 years later, we all get to witness,

00:39:59.605 --> 00:40:00.689
we all had to witness,

00:40:00.689 --> 00:40:05.111
I should say, we all witnessed a Black
man being lynched for the same thing

00:40:05.111 --> 00:40:07.780
that he was protesting 52 years ago.

00:40:07.780 --> 00:40:09.281
So we can make an argument, right?

00:40:09.281 --> 00:40:13.202
Civil Rights Act gets
signed into law in 1964.

00:40:13.202 --> 00:40:17.206
We can make an argument that things
are marginally, marginally better,

00:40:17.206 --> 00:40:20.376
better for Black people in America.

00:40:20.376 --> 00:40:20.918
But,

00:40:20.918 --> 00:40:21.836
I'm 35.

00:40:21.877 --> 00:40:26.132
If I say in 52 years that things
are marginally better than they

00:40:26.132 --> 00:40:32.179
were 52 years ago for Black people
in America, then I failed. And we’ve all failed.

00:40:32.179 --> 00:40:32.972
- Absolutely.
- We’ve all failed.

00:40:32.972 --> 00:40:33.305
- Absolutely.

00:40:33.347 --> 00:40:36.976
And I, and I would encourage, there's a
lot of really great articles about the

00:40:36.976 --> 00:40:39.520
white allyship moment between all three.

00:40:39.520 --> 00:40:42.440
Like there's very longform articles
about that, that I encourage

00:40:42.440 --> 00:40:44.775
everyone to read. And you're right.

00:40:44.775 --> 00:40:46.610
It's just crazy that,

00:40:46.610 --> 00:40:51.115
yes, that was so long ago, but it's
even wild that we were dealing with.

00:40:51.157 --> 00:40:56.203
Like, my mom was born at that time,
you know, so, but you're right.

00:40:56.203 --> 00:40:59.748
I think it's gonna take an
accumulation of moments like that

